<Layout title="About — Jack Bush">
    <main>
        <section>
            <h1>I've been doing this long enough to have made most of the mistakes already.</h1>
            <p>I started out as an industrial designer in New Zealand, then moved to London and spent a decade working across digital product — designing, building, managing, leading. I've been the person making things and the person responsible for the team making things and, at OVO and Circulor, the person responsible for the people responsible for the team making things. Each vantage point is useful.</p>
            <p>My background is a bit unusual. Industrial design training means I think about problems physically before I think about them digitally — how does this actually work in someone's hands, in someone's day? A stint in full-stack development means I'm not guessing when I'm talking to engineers. A few years as a photographer means I notice things.</p>
            <p>I work best at the early-to-mid stage — seed to Series B or so — where the product is still being figured out. I'm less useful once it's optimisation all the way down. I like the bit where you're still working out what you're building, and the decisions you make now actually change what it becomes.</p>
            <p>I live in the south of France. I keep slightly odd hours as a result, which some clients find useful and others find mildly alarming.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Something I actually believe</h2>
            <p>The best product people I've worked with share one thing: they can tell the difference between what customers say they want and what they're actually trying to do. That gap is where the real work is. It's harder than it sounds and worth getting right before you build anything.</p>
        </section>
    </main>
</Layout>
